{"context":"EKA is consumed by humans and by AI agents (ADR-022/ADR-023: the skill pack installs into agent configurations; `eka discuss` / `eka execute` are agent workflows). Both audiences hit shortcomings of the tool itself: confusing refusals, missing commands, rough edges, ideas for improvement. Today there is **no channel** for that feedback — it dies in the session or requires the user to manually open a GitHub issue in the repository (a manual, discoverability-poor step that agents cannot perform at all).\n\nThe module is now right for three reasons. First, the skill pack already makes agents the primary daily consumers of the CLI — the population that finds the most shortcomings and has the least ability to report them (no browser, no GitHub UI). Second, GitHub integration precedent exists in the binary: `eka update` (ADR-024) already speaks the GitHub API with the deterministic refusal / exit-code / `--yes` interaction model; issue creation is a direct extension of that surface. Third, the binary already carries build-time metadata via `-ldflags` (`cmd.version`, ADR-024), so a build-time injection path for a publish credential is established plumbing, not new infrastructure.\n\nFeedback is **not engineering knowledge**: it is meta-information about the tool, addressed at the EKA maintainers, and never a unit of the repository or the canonical store. Storing it in the workspace home (outside any repository) keeps the knowledge model clean and gives the user a durable local record of what they reported and where it landed.","decision":"1. **Feedback lives as plain files under `EKA_HOME/feedback/\u003cid\u003e.md`** — YAML frontmatter + markdown body (the ADR document shape, which is also the natural GitHub issue body). Feedback is **never** a CKO and **never** enters the canonical store (`eka.db`); the workspace `drafts/` tree precedent (ADR-019 D8) applies — home-area storage, repository-independent. The file carries the full triage record:\n\n   ```yaml\n   id: fbk-20260812-\u003cslug\u003e\n   type: bug|suggestion|improvement|question\n   title: ...\n   severity: low|medium|high\n   source: human|agent\n   eka_version: \u003ccmd.version\u003e   # auto-injected\n   os: \u003cGOOS\u003e/\u003cGOARCH\u003e          # auto-injected\n   command: \u003cinvoked command\u003e   # auto-injected\n   status: draft|published\n   issue_url: ...               # written by publish\n   issue_number: ...            # written by publish\n   created: YYYY-MM-DD\n   ```\n\n2. **`eka feedback` is a new command group** — thin Cobra layer (client-only boundary: production `cmd/` code imports neither `store`, `workspace`, nor `sync`; the home path resolves through the existing `runtime.HomeDir()`). Subcommands:\n\n   - `eka feedback new --type \u003ct\u003e --title \"\u003ct\u003e\" [--severity \u003cs\u003e] [--source human|agent] [--content-file \u003cf\u003e] [--json]` — writes the draft file; `--type`/`--title` required (usage exit 2 otherwise, mirroring `eka note`'s `--role`); missing `--content-file` scaffolds the per-type empty body (bug: description/steps/expected/actual; others: description); auto-injects `eka_version` (`cmd.version`), `os`, `command`, `created`.\n   - `eka feedback publish \u003cid\u003e [--yes] [--json]` — creates the GitHub issue, then rewrites the file: `status: published`, `issue_number`, `issue_url`. Idempotent: an already-published feedback refuses (exit 1). Deterministic interaction: a non-TTY invocation without `--yes` refuses (exit 2) — a piped run never blocks on an invisible prompt (ADR-024 pattern).\n   - `eka feedback list [--json]` — all local feedback with id / type / title / status / issue URL; deterministic ordering; exit 0 even when empty.\n\n   Exit codes: `0` success · `1` refusal (unknown id class 2, already-published, empty/unbundled token, network, API error) · `2` usage. Machine reports follow the `eka-note-v1` schema convention (`eka-feedback-new-v1`, `eka-feedback-publish-v1`, `eka-feedback-list-v1`); human output follows the CLI house style (`✓` `•` `→` icons, no emoji, deterministic when piped).\n\n3. **Publishing targets a fixed repository: `maleolabs/engineering-knowledge-architecture`, via the GitHub REST API** (`POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues`), with a **token bundled into the release binary at build time**. The token is a **fine-grained PAT scoped to exactly one repository with `issues: write` only** — never a classic PAT (which spans all repositories). Injection reuses the `-ldflags` path: `-X github.com/maleolabs/engineering-knowledge-architecture/feedback.issueToken=${FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN}` in `.anvil/pipelines/build.yaml` (all six platforms); `.github/workflows/release.yml` passes `FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN }}` to the build step; the token never appears in logs or repository text. Dev/test/CI builds leave the variable empty (default `\"\"`), and `publish` then refuses deterministically with a release-binary hint (the ADR-024 `version == \"dev\"` analogue). A bundled token is **public by construction** (extractable from the released binary) — the decision is accepted because the blast radius is deliberately minimal: the token can only create issues on one repository, nothing else; consequences and mitigations are recorded below.\n\n4. **The issue body is the rendered feedback file** — frontmatter fields as the report header (type, severity, source, `eka_version`, `os`, `command`) plus the markdown body. Version/OS/command metadata is what makes a one-line agent report triageable without a conversation.\n\n5. **The skill pack gains `eka-feedback`** — a new skill in `skills/manifest.yaml` teaching agents when and how to report (quality bar: actionable, no known duplicates, include what was attempted), wired into `eka-engineering-workflow` as a **conscious end-of-work step** (after finishing a job, if the agent hit an EKA shortcoming: write + publish). Deliberately **not automatic**: feedback is a decision, not a side effect.","consequences":"- **Positive — zero-config reporting.** No token setup for humans or agents: `eka feedback new` + `eka feedback publish --yes` works out of the box on any release binary. Agents get a channel they previously did not have.\n- **Positive — durable local record.** The user keeps every report with its issue link; `status` makes the draft/published split explicit; nothing depends on remembering what was filed.\n- **Positive — triage-ready issues.** Every issue carries version, OS, command, source, severity — the metadata `eka update`-style maintainers need, injected without user effort.\n- **Negative — the bundled token is public.** Anyone can create issues on the repository as the bot identity. Accepted for v1 because the blast radius is a single repository's issue tracker; the repository remains in control (labels, close, moderation). Mitigations: fine-grained `issues: write` only (no code/release/other-repo access), no-expiry-free trade-off — the fine-grained PAT must carry an expiry (max 1 year), so **token renewal is a scheduled maintenance item**; an expired token fails closed with a clear refusal until the next release.\n- **Negative — shared rate limit.** All users share the token's API quota (5,000 requests/hour). Issue creation is one POST per publish — negligible at current scale; the shared-quota failure mode is documented and the GitHub App endpoint (Future Extensibility) is the exit.\n- **Negative — no per-user attribution on the issue.** Issues are authored by the bot identity; the submitter is identified only by `source` and free-form body text. Accepted: EKA issues are triaged on content, not submitter.\n- **Risks.** Token revocation by GitHub secret scanning (mitigated: single-repo scope limits damage; fail-fast messaging; renewal cadence); an issue is created twice from different machines (mitigated: per-file status guard; cross-user deduplication is deferred — Future Extensibility).\n\n### Future Extensibility (not implemented)\n\n- **GitHub App + endpoint** — short-lived (1-hour) installation tokens, per-submitter identity, central abuse control, no public long-lived secret; the documented exit when scale or abuse demands it.\n- **Pre-publish duplicate detection** — GitHub issue search on title before creating, with a match report instead of a blind create.\n- **Context attachment** — auto-include the active project/repository identity and the failing command's exact invocation in the issue body.\n- **List filters** — `--type`, `--status`, `--json` pagination.","alternativesConsidered":"- **User-supplied credential (`GITHUB_TOKEN` env / `gh` CLI)** — rejected: every human and every agent environment must configure a credential before the channel works; agent environments routinely have neither. The zero-config property is the point of the module.\n- **GitHub App + backend endpoint issuing short-lived installation tokens** — rejected for this milestone: requires external infrastructure (hosted endpoint, app registration, abuse control) outside the repository's control; the long-term exit when the shared-token model stops scaling (Future Extensibility).\n- **Feedback in the canonical store / a repository** — rejected: feedback is not engineering knowledge; coupling it to a repository or `eka.db` pollutes the knowledge model and binds reports to a project they do not belong to.\n- **Structured JSON files** — rejected: markdown + frontmatter is human-writable, agent-writable, diffable, and is directly reusable as the GitHub issue body."}